--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- V1__baseline_schema.sql
--
-- SINGLE SOURCE OF TRUTH for the GeoWealth "GP" application schema.
--
-- Provenance : extracted from PRODUCTION  192.168.1.42 : 1521 / ORCL12VM
--              schema GP, Oracle 19c, via DBMS_METADATA.GET_DDL.
-- Generated  : 2026-06-01.
--
-- Portability transforms applied during extraction:
--   EMIT_SCHEMA=false        -> no "GP". owner prefix; objects are created in
--                               whatever schema the migration connects as.
--   SEGMENT_ATTRIBUTES=false -> no tablespace / physical storage clauses.
--   STORAGE=false, TABLESPACE=false.
--   PRETTY=true, SQLTERMINATOR=true.
--
-- Object order is dependency-safe: types -> sequences -> tables (with inline
-- PK/UNIQUE/CHECK) -> indexes -> foreign keys -> views -> functions ->
-- procedures -> packages -> triggers -> synonyms. Views and PL/SQL are emitted
-- as CREATE OR REPLACE [FORCE]; any object that is invalid purely because of
-- creation order is fixed by the DBMS_UTILITY.COMPILE_SCHEMA call at the end.
--
-- Deliberately EXCLUDED from this baseline (see db/optional/env_objects.sql):
--   * database links  (passwords are not extractable from the data dictionary)
--   * DBMS_SCHEDULER jobs
--   * materialized views
--   * Java classes (loaded as compiled binaries; no JAVA SOURCE to extract -
--     they must be reloaded with loadjava from the original jar)
-- Also excluded: ISEQ$$ identity sequences (auto-created by IDENTITY columns)
-- and constraint-backed indexes (auto-created by their PK/UNIQUE constraints).
--
-- TARGET SCHEMA: object DDL is owner-agnostic, but a handful of PL/SQL bodies,
-- one dynamic-SQL string and one trigger ON-clause still carry hardcoded
-- "GP". references (e.g. a "GP"."custodianArr" collection-type parameter, an
-- INSERT INTO "GP"."SCHWAB_FILE_STATUS_TBL"). They resolve correctly when the
-- migration is applied INTO a schema named GP - the canonical target.
--
-- PREREQUISITE - MAX_STRING_SIZE=EXTENDED: the schema uses VARCHAR2 columns up
-- to 32767 and per-column COLLATE clauses, both of which require the target PDB
-- to be in EXTENDED mode. On a fresh Oracle Free PDB, as SYSDBA:
--   ALTER PLUGGABLE DATABASE <pdb> CLOSE; ALTER PLUGGABLE DATABASE <pdb> OPEN UPGRADE;
--   ALTER SYSTEM SET MAX_STRING_SIZE=EXTENDED; @?/rdbms/admin/utl32k.sql
--   ALTER PLUGGABLE DATABASE <pdb> CLOSE; ALTER PLUGGABLE DATABASE <pdb> OPEN;
-- Without this, every COLLATE / extended-VARCHAR2 table fails with ORA-43929.
--
-- HOW TO APPLY: Flyway OSS's lightweight parser cannot handle this volume of
-- enterprise PL/SQL (it miscounts block depth in pipelined packages). Apply the
-- file with sqlplus / SQLcl (the native Oracle script runners this format
-- targets), then register it with `flyway baseline -baselineVersion=1`.
-- System-generated SYS_C##### constraint names were stripped during extraction
-- so Oracle assigns fresh ones (avoids ORA-02264 collisions on a new database).
--
-- This file is GENERATED. Do not hand-edit; re-extract instead.
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------


